{
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Kestrix",
          "version": "0.1.0",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "py.exec.eval",
              "name": "PyExecEval",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "eval() of a value that is not a literal"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "eval runs its argument as Python code. If any part of it comes from a user, a file or the network, an attacker can run arbitrary code."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Parse data instead of evaluating it: ast.literal_eval for Python literals, json.loads for JSON, or an explicit lookup table.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** eval runs its argument as Python code. If any part of it comes from a user, a file or the network, an attacker can run arbitrary code.\n\n**Fix:** Parse data instead of evaluating it: ast.literal_eval for Python literals, json.loads for JSON, or an explicit lookup table."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-95"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.exec.exec",
              "name": "PyExecExec",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "exec() of a value that is not a literal"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "exec runs its argument as Python statements, so attacker-influenced text becomes attacker-controlled code."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Remove the dynamic code path. Dispatch to known functions with a dict, or load plugins from a fixed allowlist of modules.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** exec runs its argument as Python statements, so attacker-influenced text becomes attacker-controlled code.\n\n**Fix:** Remove the dynamic code path. Dispatch to known functions with a dict, or load plugins from a fixed allowlist of modules."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-95"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.exec.compile",
              "name": "PyExecCompile",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "compile() of a value that is not a literal"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "compile turns text into a code object that is usually executed next; building it from untrusted text allows code injection."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Avoid compiling dynamic source. If you need an expression language, parse it with ast and evaluate only allowed node types.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** compile turns text into a code object that is usually executed next; building it from untrusted text allows code injection.\n\n**Fix:** Avoid compiling dynamic source. If you need an expression language, parse it with ast and evaluate only allowed node types."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-95"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.subprocess.shell_true",
              "name": "PySubprocessShellTrue",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Command run through a shell"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "With shell=True the command is parsed by /bin/sh. Any interpolated value can add ';', '&&' or '$(...)' and run other commands."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Pass the command as a list of arguments without shell=True, for example subprocess.run(['git', 'log', ref], check=True). Use shlex.quote only when a shell is unavoidable.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** With shell=True the command is parsed by /bin/sh. Any interpolated value can add ';', '&&' or '$(...)' and run other commands.\n\n**Fix:** Pass the command as a list of arguments without shell=True, for example subprocess.run(['git', 'log', ref], check=True). Use shlex.quote only when a shell is unavoidable."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.os.system",
              "name": "PyOsSystem",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "os.system or os.popen with a built command"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "os.system and os.popen always run the string through a shell, so interpolated values can inject commands."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use subprocess.run with a list of arguments and no shell, and check the return code.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** os.system and os.popen always run the string through a shell, so interpolated values can inject commands.\n\n**Fix:** Use subprocess.run with a list of arguments and no shell, and check the return code."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.deserialize.pickle",
              "name": "PyDeserializePickle",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unsafe deserialization (pickle, dill, shelve, marshal)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Loading a pickle-style stream can run arbitrary code chosen by whoever wrote the bytes. It is never safe on untrusted data."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Store and exchange data as JSON (or another data-only format) and validate it, for example with a Pydantic model.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Loading a pickle-style stream can run arbitrary code chosen by whoever wrote the bytes. It is never safe on untrusted data.\n\n**Fix:** Store and exchange data as JSON (or another data-only format) and validate it, for example with a Pydantic model."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.deserialize.yaml_unsafe",
              "name": "PyDeserializeYamlUnsafe",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "YAML loaded with a loader that can build objects"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The full and unsafe PyYAML loaders can construct arbitrary Python objects from tags in the document, which leads to code execution."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use yaml.safe_load, or yaml.load(data, Loader=yaml.SafeLoader).",
                "markdown": "**Why:** The full and unsafe PyYAML loaders can construct arbitrary Python objects from tags in the document, which leads to code execution.\n\n**Fix:** Use yaml.safe_load, or yaml.load(data, Loader=yaml.SafeLoader)."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.sql.string_built",
              "name": "PySqlStringBuilt",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SQL statement built from strings"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Formatting values into SQL text lets a value close the string and change the query (SQL injection)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use bound parameters: cursor.execute('SELECT * FROM t WHERE id = ?', (item_id,)), or the placeholders of your driver or ORM.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Formatting values into SQL text lets a value close the string and change the query (SQL injection).\n\n**Fix:** Use bound parameters: cursor.execute('SELECT * FROM t WHERE id = ?', (item_id,)), or the placeholders of your driver or ORM."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.tls.verify_disabled",
              "name": "PyTlsVerifyDisabled",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "TLS certificate or hostname verification disabled"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Without verification anyone on the network path can impersonate the server and read or change the traffic."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Keep verification on. For a private CA, pass its bundle (verify='/path/ca.pem' or ssl.create_default_context(cafile=...)).",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Without verification anyone on the network path can impersonate the server and read or change the traffic.\n\n**Fix:** Keep verification on. For a private CA, pass its bundle (verify='/path/ca.pem' or ssl.create_default_context(cafile=...))."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-295"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.crypto.weak_hash",
              "name": "PyCryptoWeakHash",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "MD5 or SHA-1 hash"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "MD5 and SHA-1 have practical collision attacks and must not protect integrity, signatures or passwords."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use hashlib.sha256 (or hashlib.blake2b). For passwords use a slow KDF such as hashlib.scrypt. If the hash is only a checksum or an identifier, pass usedforsecurity=False.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** MD5 and SHA-1 have practical collision attacks and must not protect integrity, signatures or passwords.\n\n**Fix:** Use hashlib.sha256 (or hashlib.blake2b). For passwords use a slow KDF such as hashlib.scrypt. If the hash is only a checksum or an identifier, pass usedforsecurity=False."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-328"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.crypto.insecure_random",
              "name": "PyCryptoInsecureRandom",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "random module used for a secret value"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The random module is a predictable generator. Tokens, keys, salts and passwords made with it can be guessed."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use the secrets module: secrets.token_urlsafe(32), secrets.token_hex(16) or secrets.choice.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** The random module is a predictable generator. Tokens, keys, salts and passwords made with it can be guessed.\n\n**Fix:** Use the secrets module: secrets.token_urlsafe(32), secrets.token_hex(16) or secrets.choice."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-330"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.tempfile.mktemp",
              "name": "PyTempfileMktemp",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "tempfile.mktemp returns a name, not a file"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Another process can create the file between mktemp choosing the name and your code opening it, and redirect your writes."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use tempfile.mkstemp, tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile or tempfile.TemporaryDirectory, which create the file atomically.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Another process can create the file between mktemp choosing the name and your code opening it, and redirect your writes.\n\n**Fix:** Use tempfile.mkstemp, tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile or tempfile.TemporaryDirectory, which create the file atomically."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-377"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.flask.debug",
              "name": "PyFlaskDebug",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Debug mode enabled"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Debug mode shows stack traces and, in Flask, an interactive console that runs code for anyone who can reach the page."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Read the setting from the environment and keep it off in deployed code, for example app.run(debug=os.environ.get('FLASK_DEBUG') == '1').",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Debug mode shows stack traces and, in Flask, an interactive console that runs code for anyone who can reach the page.\n\n**Fix:** Read the setting from the environment and keep it off in deployed code, for example app.run(debug=os.environ.get('FLASK_DEBUG') == '1')."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-489"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.jwt.no_verify",
              "name": "PyJwtNoVerify",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "JWT decoded without verifying the signature"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A token whose signature is not checked can be forged by anyone, so its claims (user, role, expiry) mean nothing."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Verify the signature with an explicit algorithm list, for example jwt.decode(token, key, algorithms=['RS256']). Never allow 'none'.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** A token whose signature is not checked can be forged by anyone, so its claims (user, role, expiry) mean nothing.\n\n**Fix:** Verify the signature with an explicit algorithm list, for example jwt.decode(token, key, algorithms=['RS256']). Never allow 'none'."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-347"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.xml.unsafe_parser",
              "name": "PyXmlUnsafeParser",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Standard-library XML parser on data that is not a literal"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The standard-library XML parsers are not hardened against every hostile document (entity expansion, very large inputs)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "For untrusted XML use defusedxml (defusedxml.ElementTree.fromstring), and bound the input size.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** The standard-library XML parsers are not hardened against every hostile document (entity expansion, very large inputs).\n\n**Fix:** For untrusted XML use defusedxml (defusedxml.ElementTree.fromstring), and bound the input size."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "precision": "low",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-776"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.assert.security",
              "name": "PyAssertSecurity",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "assert used for an access check"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Python removes assert statements when run with -O, so the check silently disappears in optimized deployments."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use an explicit check: if not user.is_admin: raise PermissionError(...).",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Python removes assert statements when run with -O, so the check silently disappears in optimized deployments.\n\n**Fix:** Use an explicit check: if not user.is_admin: raise PermissionError(...)."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-617"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.path.traversal",
              "name": "PyPathTraversal",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "File path built from request data"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A value such as '../../etc/passwd' in a request parameter can escape the intended directory and read or overwrite other files."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Reduce the name with werkzeug.utils.secure_filename or os.path.basename, or resolve the path and check it stays under the base directory (Path.resolve().is_relative_to(base)). Flask's send_from_directory does this for you.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** A value such as '../../etc/passwd' in a request parameter can escape the intended directory and read or overwrite other files.\n\n**Fix:** Reduce the name with werkzeug.utils.secure_filename or os.path.basename, or resolve the path and check it stays under the base directory (Path.resolve().is_relative_to(base)). Flask's send_from_directory does this for you."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-22"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.ssrf.request",
              "name": "PySsrfRequest",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "HTTP request to a URL taken from request data or model output"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When a user (or a model's output) chooses the URL, the server can be made to call internal services, cloud metadata endpoints or other hosts on the attacker's behalf."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Fix the scheme and host in code and let the input choose only a path or query value, or check the parsed host against an allowlist before the request. Do not follow redirects to other hosts.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** When a user (or a model's output) chooses the URL, the server can be made to call internal services, cloud metadata endpoints or other hosts on the attacker's behalf.\n\n**Fix:** Fix the scheme and host in code and let the input choose only a path or query value, or check the parsed host against an allowlist before the request. Do not follow redirects to other hosts."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-918"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.redirect.open",
              "name": "PyRedirectOpen",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Redirect to a location taken from request data"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A redirect target taken from the request lets a link on your domain send users to a phishing site that looks trusted."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Redirect to a path you build (url_for, reverse) or check the target first: allow only relative paths on your own host, for example with Django's url_has_allowed_host_and_scheme.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** A redirect target taken from the request lets a link on your domain send users to a phishing site that looks trusted.\n\n**Fix:** Redirect to a path you build (url_for, reverse) or check the target first: allow only relative paths on your own host, for example with Django's url_has_allowed_host_and_scheme."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-601"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.xss.markup",
              "name": "PyXssMarkup",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Request data marked as safe HTML or used as a template"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Markup and mark_safe switch off autoescaping, so request data in them is rendered as HTML and script (XSS). As the source of render_template_string it is a template, and template syntax in it runs on the server (SSTI, CWE-1336)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Pass the value to the template as data and let autoescaping handle it, or escape it first with markupsafe.escape or html.escape. Never build a template's source from input; use render_template with a fixed template file.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Markup and mark_safe switch off autoescaping, so request data in them is rendered as HTML and script (XSS). As the source of render_template_string it is a template, and template syntax in it runs on the server (SSTI, CWE-1336).\n\n**Fix:** Pass the value to the template as data and let autoescaping handle it, or escape it first with markupsafe.escape or html.escape. Never build a template's source from input; use render_template with a fixed template file."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.llm.output_to_sink",
              "name": "PyLlmOutputToSink",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "LLM output used as code, a shell command, SQL or a file path"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A model's output follows whatever text reached its prompt. A prompt injection hidden in a web page, a document or a user message can make the model emit code, a command, SQL or a path, and this sink runs it with your program's permissions."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Treat model output as untrusted input. Parse it into a fixed structure (JSON validated against a schema, or a choice from an allowlist), pass values as arguments rather than code or command text, and use bound SQL parameters and basename-checked paths.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** A model's output follows whatever text reached its prompt. A prompt injection hidden in a web page, a document or a user message can make the model emit code, a command, SQL or a path, and this sink runs it with your program's permissions.\n\n**Fix:** Treat model output as untrusted input. Parse it into a fixed structure (JSON validated against a schema, or a choice from an allowlist), pass values as arguments rather than code or command text, and use bound SQL parameters and basename-checked paths."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-94"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.ml.unsafe_model_load",
              "name": "PyMlUnsafeModelLoad",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Model loaded in a way that can run code from the file"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Pickle-based model files (torch.load without weights_only, joblib, pickle) and Keras files with safe_mode=False can run arbitrary code when loaded, and trust_remote_code=True runs Python shipped with a downloaded model. A tampered or malicious model file is enough."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Load weights only: torch.load(path, weights_only=True), or safetensors. Keep Keras safe_mode on. Load joblib or pickle models only from files you produced and verify their hash. Leave trust_remote_code off, or pin the model revision you reviewed.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Pickle-based model files (torch.load without weights_only, joblib, pickle) and Keras files with safe_mode=False can run arbitrary code when loaded, and trust_remote_code=True runs Python shipped with a downloaded model. A tampered or malicious model file is enough.\n\n**Fix:** Load weights only: torch.load(path, weights_only=True), or safetensors. Keep Keras safe_mode on. Load joblib or pickle models only from files you produced and verify their hash. Leave trust_remote_code off, or pin the model revision you reviewed."
              },
